> Now I'm just curious about the semantic difference between "other than" and
> "and".
>
>
>
> I think it's an issue of topicality.  "X other than Y" reduces the
> topicality of Y, almost to the point of dismissal, in relation to X, while
> "X and Y" treats the two as almost equal, with only the fact of X occurring
> before Y to indicate that X is slightly more topical than Y.
